  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES-East Nighttime Microphysics RGB imagery shows a \nmixture of high clouds streaming in from the west associated with a \nshortwave causing thunderstorms in Nebraska and South Dakota as well \nas low stratus and at least some patches of FG (confirmed by METARs \nreporting FG at SAW as well as others around the region). This \ncombination of cloud types should keep low temperatures from falling \nout of the 40s this morning except for a few isolated typical cold \nspots. Fog is not anticipated to become widespread. \n\nThe 500mb pattern consists of 599 dam ridging over Arkansas bounded \nby deep troughs centered over the Pacific Northwest, the Bahamas, \nand northern Quebec. The aforementioned ridge-riding shortwave \ncurrently over western South Dakota will track a surface low south \nof the UP this weekend and bring chances of rain to the southern UP \ntoday and Saturday. The trend of rainfall southward continued in a \nbig way over the past 24 hours, with 00Z HREF showing \nmeaningful (15+%) probabilities of even a half a tenth of an \ninch of rain by 00Z Sunday being limited to the immediate \nvicinity of the state line and Menominee County south of US-2. \nThe 12Z LREF does at least shift some ~20% chances of measurable\nrainfall a bit further into the UP, but impactful rainfall will\nbe certainly south of the UP as even the 90th percentile of the\nLREF (reasonable worst case) is less than an inch even for the \nMenominee area (and less than a third of an inch for all of the \nUP except for Menominee County south of US-2). With the drying \ntrend in the forecast, especially with easterly flow descending \noff the Canadian Shield, overnight lows into Saturday morning \nlook to be around the mid 30s in the interior east and in some \nisolated locations of the interior west, which would lead to \nfrost potential (20-40% chance, especially for elevated \nsurfaces). Those in the eastern UP with sensitive vegetation \noutdoors should monitor the forecast as the trend has been \ncolder, but if cloud cover from the southern disturbance extends\nfurther north than currently forecast, temperatures may not \nfall enough for frost formation.\n\nIn the wake of the disturbance, the western CONUS trough will shift \ninto the Northern Plains Sunday, weakening slightly. At the same \ntime, ridging to the north of the trough amplifies, causing split \nflow. At this time, the favored solution is for the UP to be \naffected by the northern branch of this split flow which will lead \nto persistent high pressure and dry weather and near-normal to \nslightly below normal temperatures for Sunday through at least the \nfirst few days of next week until ensemble spread increases for the \nmidweek and beyond. The CPC 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks both show \n50+% chances for the UP to be in above average temperatures for the \nend of September and a slight favoring of below normal precipitation \n(30-40% chance).",
